From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen.kooi@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 20:10:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] test: drop always-false size_t overflow check in
 MLValueToTensorProto

MLValueToTensorProto() takes the element count as a size_t and then
checks whether it exceeds SIZE_MAX. A size_t can never exceed SIZE_MAX,
so the check is dead code on every target. On targets where size_t is
32 bits wide, GCC can prove it and reports
"comparison is always false due to limited range of data type"
[-Wtype-limits]; with onnxruntime's COMPILE_WARNING_AS_ERROR that breaks
the onnx_test_runner build for 32-bit Arm.

The similar check in the tensor-loading path above it compares an
int64_t element count and is a real overflow guard on 32-bit targets,
so it stays.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Koen Kooi <koen.kooi@oss.qualcomm.com>
---
 onnxruntime/test/onnx/tensorprotoutils.cc | 3 ---
 1 file changed, 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/onnxruntime/test/onnx/tensorprotoutils.cc b/onnxruntime/test/onnx/tensorprotoutils.cc
index d6b10be..5f70ab1 100644
--- a/onnxruntime/test/onnx/tensorprotoutils.cc
+++ b/onnxruntime/test/onnx/tensorprotoutils.cc
@@ -758,9 +758,6 @@ Status MLValueToTensorProto(Ort::Value& value, onnx::TensorProto& tensor_proto)
     tensor_proto.add_dims(out_shape[j]);
   }
   size_t tensor_size = tensor_info.GetElementCount();
-  if (static_cast<uint64_t>(tensor_size) > SIZE_MAX) {
-    return Status(common::ONNXRUNTIME, common::INVALID_ARGUMENT, "Size overflow");
-  }
 
   ONNXTensorElementDataType tensor_elem_data_type = tensor_info.GetElementType();
   int tensor_elem_bytes;
